{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VR8AuYPm5JkGGrQeE7FnGQ","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→id: prd002-sys\n     2→title: \"pkg/sys: Syscall Abstractions\"\n     3→\n     4→problem: |\n     5→  Four utilities (ls, du, find, xargs) need OS-level capabilities that Go's standard\n     6→  library does not expose in a portable, stable form: terminal-width queries, extended\n     7→  file metadata (hard-link count, device and inode numbers, physical block counts),\n     8→  and signal handling for SIGPIPE and SIGWINCH. On Darwin and Linux these syscalls\n     9→  diverge in struct layout (st_mtimespec vs st_mtim, st_blocks interpretation) and\n    10→  available interfaces (TIOCGWINSZ ioctl). Without pkg/sys each cmd/ package would\n    11→  import syscall or golang.org/x/sys directly, duplicating the Darwin/Linux divergence\n    12→  handling and making it harder to test or audit the syscall surface.\n    13→\n    14→  pkg/sys provides a stable, tested interface for these three capability areas. Each\n    15→  capability is driven by at least one concrete utility requirement derived from C source\n    16→  analysis (see dependency-map.yaml).\n    17→\n    18→goals:\n    19→  - G1: Provide a portable terminal-width query and SIGPIPE handler that work on Darwin and Linux without cgo.\n    20→  - G2: Expose extended file metadata (st_nlink, st_uid, st_gid, st_rdev, st_blocks, st_ino, st_dev) not available from os.FileInfo.\n    21→  - G3: Provide a SIGWINCH handler that calls a user-supplied callback when the terminal is resized.\n    22→\n    23→requirements:\n    24→  R1:\n    25→    title: Terminal width and TTY detection\n    26→    items:\n    27→      - R1.1: Must provide func TerminalWidth() (int, error) that returns the current terminal column count by calling ioctl(TIOCGWINSZ) on stdout. Returns an error (not panic) when stdout is not a terminal or when ioctl fails.\n    28→      - R1.2: Must use golang.org/x/sys/unix (the project's only external dependency) to perform the TIOCGWINSZ ioctl; must not shell out to tput or stty and must not import golang.org/x/term.\n    29→      - R1.3: |\n    30→          Must provide func IsTerminal(fd uintptr) bool that returns true when the file\n    31→          descriptor refers to a terminal, and false otherwise. Implementation: call\n    32→          golang.org/x/sys/unix.IoctlGetWinsize(int(fd), unix.TIOCGWINSZ) and return\n    33→          err == nil. Returns false for pipes, regular files, and non-terminal descriptors.\n    34→          The caller (pkg/format ColorEnabled) is responsible for extracting the fd from an\n    35→          io.Writer via type assertion to *os.File. Required by pkg/format to suppress color\n    36→          output when stdout is redirected.\n    37→      - R1.4: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:2281): ls uses struct winsize to determine column count for its default multi-column layout and for the -w/--width flag default.'\n    38→      - R1.5: |\n    39→          Must provide func InstallSIGPIPEHandler() that installs a SIGPIPE handler causing the\n    40→          process to exit 0 when stdout or stderr is closed by a downstream consumer.\n    41→          Implementation: use signal.Notify(c, syscall.SIGPIPE) with a buffered channel of\n    42→          size 1; in a dedicated goroutine call os.Exit(0) when the channel receives. Must not\n    43→          use signal.Ignore so that deferred cleanup functions do not run on SIGPIPE (matching\n    44→          GNU behavior where SIGPIPE terminates silently).\n    45→      - R1.6: InstallSIGPIPEHandler must be safe to call multiple times; only one goroutine per invocation is started.\n    46→      - R1.7: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:1600-1620): ls installs a SIGPIPE handler via sigaction so that piping ls output to head does not produce a broken-pipe error message. du, find, and xargs have the same requirement.'\n    47→\n    48→  R2:\n    49→    title: Extended file metadata\n    50→    items:\n    51→      - R2.1: Must provide func Stat(path string) (*FileInfo, error) and func Lstat(path string) (*FileInfo, error). Stat follows symbolic links (equivalent to os.Stat); Lstat does not follow symbolic links (equivalent to os.Lstat). Both return a *FileInfo populated from syscall.Stat_t.\n    52→      - R2.2: |\n    53→          FileInfo must be defined as the following named struct in package sys:\n    54→\n    55→            type FileInfo struct {\n    56→                Mode    os.FileMode // file mode and type bits (from syscall.Stat_t.Mode)\n    57→                Size    int64       // apparent file size in bytes (st_size)\n    58→                Nlink   uint64      // hard-link count (st_nlink)\n    59→                Uid     uint32      // owner user ID (st_uid)\n    60→                Gid     uint32      // owner group ID (st_gid)\n    61→                Rdev    uint64      // device ID for special files (st_rdev)\n    62→                Dev     uint64      // device ID of the containing filesystem (st_dev)\n    63→                Ino     uint64      // inode number (st_ino)\n    64→                Blocks  int64       // number of 512-byte blocks allocated (st_blocks)\n    65→                Blksize int64       // preferred I/O block size (st_blksize)\n    66→                ModTime time.Time   // modification time (st_mtime / st_mtimespec)\n    67→                Info    os.FileInfo // underlying os.FileInfo for os package compatibility\n    68→            }\n    69→      - R2.3: Must abstract the Darwin/Linux divergence in struct field names (st_mtimespec on Darwin vs st_mtim on Linux; st_blocks interpretation) so callers use a single API regardless of target platform.\n    70→      - R2.4: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:213, 371): ls needs Nlink, Uid, Gid, Rdev, Mode for -l format; Blocks for -s; Ino for -i.'\n    71→      - R2.5: 'Utility context — du (du.c:554): du needs Dev and Ino for hard-link deduplication, and Blocks for physical block-count accumulation.'\n    72→      - R2.6: 'Utility context — find (find/ftsfind.c:172): find predicates (-type, -size, -newer, -perm, -uid, -gid) require Mode, Size, ModTime, Uid, Gid, Nlink from the stat struct.'\n    73→\n    74→  R3:\n    75→    title: SIGWINCH handling\n    76→    items:\n    77→      - R3.1: |\n    78→          Must provide func OnTerminalResize(callback func(width int)) that registers a SIGWINCH handler. When the terminal is resized, the handler calls TerminalWidth() and invokes the callback with the new width. If TerminalWidth() returns an error the callback is not invoked.\n    79→      - R3.2: Must support multiple calls to OnTerminalResize; each registered callback must be called on resize. Callbacks are called in registration order.\n    80→      - R3.3: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:2281, SIGWINCH registration): ls re-queries terminal width on SIGWINCH to re-format output when the terminal window is resized during a long listing.'\n    81→\n    82→non_goals:\n    83→  - pkg/sys does not wrap all of syscall or golang.org/x/sys; it exposes only the surfaces required by the current utility batch.\n    84→  - pkg/sys does not provide file-watching or inotify/kqueue interfaces.\n    85→  - pkg/sys does not handle SIGUSR1/SIGUSR2 (find's debug output); those are deferred to the find PRD.\n    86→  - pkg/sys does not provide process-group or terminal control (TIOCGPGRP, tcsetpgrp); those are deferred to the xargs PRD.\n    87→\n    88→acceptance_criteria:\n    89→  - \"AC1: TerminalWidth() returns a positive integer when called in a test with a pseudo-terminal (golang.org/x/term's test helpers or os.Pipe() as a TTY substitute is acceptable).\"\n    90→  - \"AC2: Stat() and Lstat() on a known file return FileInfo with correct Nlink, Uid, Gid, Dev, Ino, and Blocks values as confirmed by running stat(1) on the same file.\"\n    91→  - \"AC3: Lstat() on a symlink returns the symlink's own st_mode (type bits show symlink), not the target's mode.\"\n    92→  - \"AC4: InstallSIGPIPEHandler() causes a process writing to a broken pipe to exit 0 rather than printing a write error.\"\n    93→  - \"AC5: All pkg/sys code compiles on both darwin/arm64 and linux/amd64 with no build errors and no cgo.\"\n    94→  - \"AC6: All pkg/sys code compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n    95→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4cd21a5e-992a-4826-9906-d82e92835cb4","uuid":"ac2ec7eb-6fd2-451a-9b74-c70cafa76156","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/232/docs/specs/product-requirements/prd002-sys.yaml","content":"id: prd002-sys\ntitle: \"pkg/sys: Syscall Abstractions\"\n\nproblem: |\n  Four utilities (ls, du, find, xargs) need OS-level capabilities that Go's standard\n  library does not expose in a portable, stable form: terminal-width queries, extended\n  file metadata (hard-link count, device and inode numbers, physical block counts),\n  and signal handling for SIGPIPE and SIGWINCH. On Darwin and Linux these syscalls\n  diverge in struct layout (st_mtimespec vs st_mtim, st_blocks interpretation) and\n  available interfaces (TIOCGWINSZ ioctl). Without pkg/sys each cmd/ package would\n  import syscall or golang.org/x/sys directly, duplicating the Darwin/Linux divergence\n  handling and making it harder to test or audit the syscall surface.\n\n  pkg/sys provides a stable, tested interface for these three capability areas. Each\n  capability is driven by at least one concrete utility requirement derived from C source\n  analysis (see dependency-map.yaml).\n\ngoals:\n  - G1: Provide a portable terminal-width query and SIGPIPE handler that work on Darwin and Linux without cgo.\n  - G2: Expose extended file metadata (st_nlink, st_uid, st_gid, st_rdev, st_blocks, st_ino, st_dev) not available from os.FileInfo.\n  - G3: Provide a SIGWINCH handler that calls a user-supplied callback when the terminal is resized.\n\nrequirements:\n  R1:\n    title: Terminal width and TTY detection\n    items:\n      - R1.1: Must provide func TerminalWidth() (int, error) that returns the current terminal column count by calling ioctl(TIOCGWINSZ) on stdout. Returns an error (not panic) when stdout is not a terminal or when ioctl fails.\n      - R1.2: Must use golang.org/x/sys/unix (the project's only external dependency) to perform the TIOCGWINSZ ioctl; must not shell out to tput or stty and must not import golang.org/x/term.\n      - R1.3: |\n          Must provide func IsTerminal(fd uintptr) bool that returns true when the file\n          descriptor refers to a terminal, and false otherwise. Implementation: call\n          golang.org/x/sys/unix.IoctlGetWinsize(int(fd), unix.TIOCGWINSZ) and return\n          err == nil. Returns false for pipes, regular files, and non-terminal descriptors.\n          The caller (pkg/format ColorEnabled) is responsible for extracting the fd from an\n          io.Writer via type assertion to *os.File. Required by pkg/format to suppress color\n          output when stdout is redirected.\n      - R1.4: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:2281): ls uses struct winsize to determine column count for its default multi-column layout and for the -w/--width flag default.'\n      - R1.5: |\n          Must provide func InstallSIGPIPEHandler() that installs a SIGPIPE handler causing the\n          process to exit 0 when stdout or stderr is closed by a downstream consumer.\n          Implementation: use signal.Notify(c, syscall.SIGPIPE) with a buffered channel of\n          size 1; in a dedicated goroutine call os.Exit(0) when the channel receives. Must not\n          use signal.Ignore so that deferred cleanup functions do not run on SIGPIPE (matching\n          GNU behavior where SIGPIPE terminates silently).\n      - R1.6: InstallSIGPIPEHandler must be safe to call multiple times; only one goroutine per invocation is started.\n      - R1.7: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:1600-1620): ls installs a SIGPIPE handler via sigaction so that piping ls output to head does not produce a broken-pipe error message. du, find, and xargs have the same requirement.'\n\n  R2:\n    title: Extended file metadata\n    items:\n      - R2.1: Must provide func Stat(path string) (*FileInfo, error) and func Lstat(path string) (*FileInfo, error). Stat follows symbolic links (equivalent to os.Stat); Lstat does not follow symbolic links (equivalent to os.Lstat). Both return a *FileInfo populated from syscall.Stat_t.\n      - R2.2: |\n          FileInfo must be defined as the following named struct in package sys:\n\n            type FileInfo struct {\n                Mode    os.FileMode // file mode and type bits (from syscall.Stat_t.Mode)\n                Size    int64       // apparent file size in bytes (st_size)\n                Nlink   uint64      // hard-link count (st_nlink)\n                Uid     uint32      // owner user ID (st_uid)\n                Gid     uint32      // owner group ID (st_gid)\n                Rdev    uint64      // device ID for special files (st_rdev)\n                Dev     uint64      // device ID of the containing filesystem (st_dev)\n                Ino     uint64      // inode number (st_ino)\n                Blocks  int64       // number of 512-byte blocks allocated (st_blocks)\n                Blksize int64       // preferred I/O block size (st_blksize)\n                ModTime time.Time   // modification time (st_mtime / st_mtimespec)\n                Info    os.FileInfo // underlying os.FileInfo for os package compatibility\n            }\n      - R2.3: Must abstract the Darwin/Linux divergence in struct field names (st_mtimespec on Darwin vs st_mtim on Linux; st_blocks interpretation) so callers use a single API regardless of target platform.\n      - R2.4: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:213, 371): ls needs Nlink, Uid, Gid, Rdev, Mode for -l format; Blocks for -s; Ino for -i.'\n      - R2.5: 'Utility context — du (du.c:554): du needs Dev and Ino for hard-link deduplication, and Blocks for physical block-count accumulation.'\n      - R2.6: 'Utility context — find (find/ftsfind.c:172): find predicates (-type, -size, -newer, -perm, -uid, -gid) require Mode, Size, ModTime, Uid, Gid, Nlink from the stat struct.'\n\n  R3:\n    title: SIGWINCH handling\n    items:\n      - R3.1: |\n          Must provide func OnTerminalResize(callback func(width int)) that registers a SIGWINCH handler. When the terminal is resized, the handler calls TerminalWidth() and invokes the callback with the new width. If TerminalWidth() returns an error the callback is not invoked.\n      - R3.2: Must support multiple calls to OnTerminalResize; each registered callback must be called on resize. Callbacks are called in registration order.\n      - R3.3: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:2281, SIGWINCH registration): ls re-queries terminal width on SIGWINCH to re-format output when the terminal window is resized during a long listing.'\n\nnon_goals:\n  - pkg/sys does not wrap all of syscall or golang.org/x/sys; it exposes only the surfaces required by the current utility batch.\n  - pkg/sys does not provide file-watching or inotify/kqueue interfaces.\n  - pkg/sys does not handle SIGUSR1/SIGUSR2 (find's debug output); those are deferred to the find PRD.\n  - pkg/sys does not provide process-group or terminal control (TIOCGPGRP, tcsetpgrp); those are deferred to the xargs PRD.\n\nacceptance_criteria:\n  - \"AC1: TerminalWidth() returns a positive integer when called in a test with a pseudo-terminal (golang.org/x/term's test helpers or os.Pipe() as a TTY substitute is acceptable).\"\n  - \"AC2: Stat() and Lstat() on a known file return FileInfo with correct Nlink, Uid, Gid, Dev, Ino, and Blocks values as confirmed by running stat(1) on the same file.\"\n  - \"AC3: Lstat() on a symlink returns the symlink's own st_mode (type bits show symlink), not the target's mode.\"\n  - \"AC4: InstallSIGPIPEHandler() causes a process writing to a broken pipe to exit 0 rather than printing a write error.\"\n  - \"AC5: All pkg/sys code compiles on both darwin/arm64 and linux/amd64 with no build errors and no cgo.\"\n  - \"AC6: All pkg/sys code compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n","numLines":95,"startLine":1,"totalLines":95}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018NiuvZ4GHsUioukZ7Sg7fV","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→id: test-rel00.0\n     2→title: \"Test Suite: rel00.0 — build lifecycle, cmd/version, pkg/format, pkg/sys, and pkg/testutils\"\n     3→release: rel00.0\n     4→\n     5→traces:\n     6→  - rel00.0-uc001-magefiles\n     7→  - prd011-magefiles R1\n     8→  - prd011-magefiles R2\n     9→  - prd011-magefiles R3\n    10→  - prd011-magefiles R4\n    11→  - rel00.0-uc002-format\n    12→  - prd003-format R1\n    13→  - prd003-format R2\n    14→  - prd003-format R3\n    15→  - rel00.0-uc003-sys\n    16→  - prd002-sys R1\n    17→  - prd002-sys R2\n    18→  - prd002-sys R3\n    19→  - rel00.0-uc004-testutils\n    20→  - prd001-testutils R1\n    21→  - prd001-testutils R2\n    22→  - prd001-testutils R3\n    23→  - prd001-testutils R4\n    24→  - prd001-testutils R5\n    25→\n    26→preconditions:\n    27→  - A git repository with at least one tag matching v[0-9]*.\n    28→  - golangci-lint is installed and on PATH.\n    29→  - mage is installed and on PATH.\n    30→  - configuration.yaml exists at the project root with project.binary_dir set to bin.\n    31→\n    32→test_cases:\n    33→  # --- uc001-magefiles: build lifecycle and version ---\n    34→\n    35→  - name: build_produces_version_binary\n    36→    description: mage build compiles cmd/version to bin/version.\n    37→    inputs:\n    38→      command: \"mage build\"\n    39→    expected:\n    40→      exit_code: 0\n    41→      postcondition: bin/version exists and is executable\n    42→    traces:\n    43→      - prd011-magefiles R2.1\n    44→      - prd011-magefiles R2.4\n    45→\n    46→  - name: version_prints_tag\n    47→    description: bin/version prints the latest version tag and exits 0.\n    48→    inputs:\n    49→      command: \"bin/version\"\n    50→    expected:\n    51→      exit_code: 0\n    52→      stdout_pattern: \"v[0-9]+\\\\.[0-9]+\\\\.[0-9]+\\n\"\n    53→      stderr: \"\"\n    54→    traces:\n    55→      - prd011-magefiles R1.1\n    56→      - prd011-magefiles R1.2\n    57→      - prd011-magefiles R1.3\n    58→\n    59→  - name: version_flag_identical\n    60→    description: bin/version --version produces the same output as bin/version.\n    61→    inputs:\n    62→      command: \"bin/version --version\"\n    63→    expected:\n    64→      exit_code: 0\n    65→      stdout: same as bin/version with no arguments\n    66→      stderr: \"\"\n    67→    traces:\n    68→      - prd011-magefiles R1.4\n    69→\n    70→  - name: version_v_flag_identical\n    71→    description: bin/version -v produces the same output as bin/version.\n    72→    inputs:\n    73→      command: \"bin/version -v\"\n    74→    expected:\n    75→      exit_code: 0\n    76→      stdout: same as bin/version with no arguments\n    77→      stderr: \"\"\n    78→    traces:\n    79→      - prd011-magefiles R1.4\n    80→\n    81→  - name: version_unknown_flag\n    82→    description: bin/version --bogus prints usage to stderr and exits 2.\n    83→    inputs:\n    84→      command: \"bin/version --bogus\"\n    85→    expected:\n    86→      exit_code: 2\n    87→      stdout: \"\"\n    88→      stderr_contains: \"usage\"\n    89→    traces:\n    90→      - prd011-magefiles R1.4\n    91→\n    92→  - name: version_dev_build\n    93→    description: A build without ldflags prints \"dev\".\n    94→    inputs:\n    95→      command: \"go build -o /tmp/version-dev ./cmd/version && /tmp/version-dev\"\n    96→    expected:\n    97→      exit_code: 0\n    98→      stdout: \"dev\\n\"\n    99→      stderr: \"\"\n   100→    traces:\n   101→      - prd011-magefiles R1.2\n   102→\n   103→  - name: lint_passes\n   104→    description: mage lint runs golangci-lint with no warnings.\n   105→    inputs:\n   106→      command: \"mage lint\"\n   107→    expected:\n   108→      exit_code: 0\n   109→    traces:\n   110→      - prd011-magefiles R3.1\n   111→      - prd011-magefiles R3.2\n   112→\n   113→  - name: clean_removes_bin\n   114→    description: mage clean removes the bin/ directory.\n   115→    inputs:\n   116→      command: \"mage build && mage clean\"\n   117→    expected:\n   118→      exit_code: 0\n   119→      postcondition: bin/ does not exist\n   120→    traces:\n   121→      - prd011-magefiles R4.1\n   122→\n   123→  - name: clean_idempotent\n   124→    description: mage clean succeeds when bin/ does not exist.\n   125→    inputs:\n   126→      command: \"mage clean && mage clean\"\n   127→    expected:\n   128→      exit_code: 0\n   129→    traces:\n   130→      - prd011-magefiles R4.1\n   131→\n   132→  # --- uc002-format: pkg/format output formatting ---\n   133→\n   134→  - name: columns_20_entries_80_width\n   135→    description: Columns distributes 20 filenames into a grid fitting 80 columns.\n   136→    inputs:\n   137→      function: \"format.Columns(entries, 80)\"\n   138→      entries: 20 filenames of varying lengths\n   139→    expected:\n   140→      postcondition: each row fits within 80 characters; column count is maximized\n   141→    traces:\n   142→      - prd003-format R1.1\n   143→      - prd003-format R1.4\n   144→\n   145→  - name: columns_empty_input\n   146→    description: Columns returns nil for empty input.\n   147→    inputs:\n   148→      function: \"format.Columns(nil, 80)\"\n   149→    expected:\n   150→      result: nil\n   151→    traces:\n   152→      - prd003-format R1.1\n   153→\n   154→  - name: pad_right\n   155→    description: PadRight pads a string with trailing spaces to the specified width.\n   156→    inputs:\n   157→      function: 'format.PadRight(\"abc\", 10)'\n   158→    expected:\n   159→      result: '\"abc       \"  (abc + 7 spaces)'\n   160→    traces:\n   161→      - prd003-format R1.2\n   162→\n   163→  - name: pad_left\n   164→    description: PadLeft pads a string with leading spaces to the specified width.\n   165→    inputs:\n   166→      function: 'format.PadLeft(\"abc\", 10)'\n   167→    expected:\n   168→      result: '\"       abc\"  (7 spaces + abc)'\n   169→    traces:\n   170→      - prd003-format R1.2\n   171→\n   172→  - name: display_width_multibyte\n   173→    description: Column width uses rune count, not byte count, for multi-byte strings.\n   174→    inputs:\n   175→      function: 'format.PadRight(\"café\", 10)'\n   176→    expected:\n   177→      result: '\"café      \"  (café is 4 runes, padded to 10)'\n   178→    traces:\n   179→      - prd003-format R1.3\n   180→\n   181→  - name: file_type_color_all_types\n   182→    description: FileTypeColor returns correct ANSI codes for all eight file types.\n   183→    inputs:\n   184→      function: \"format.FileTypeColor(mode) for each file type\"\n   185→      setup: \"format.SetColorEnabled(true)\"\n   186→    expected:\n   187→      directory: '\"\\033[34m\"'\n   188→      symlink: '\"\\033[36m\"'\n   189→      executable: '\"\\033[32m\"'\n   190→      block_device: '\"\\033[33;1m\"'\n   191→      char_device: '\"\\033[33;1m\"'\n   192→      socket: '\"\\033[35m\"'\n   193→      pipe: '\"\\033[33m\"'\n   194→      regular: '\"\"'\n   195→    traces:\n   196→      - prd003-format R2.1\n   197→      - prd003-format R2.4\n   198→\n   199→  - name: reset_color_enabled\n   200→    description: Reset returns ANSI reset sequence when color is enabled.\n   201→    inputs:\n   202→      function: \"format.Reset()\"\n   203→      setup: \"format.SetColorEnabled(true)\"\n   204→    expected:\n   205→      result: '\"\\033[0m\"'\n   206→    traces:\n   207→      - prd003-format R2.2\n   208→\n   209→  - name: color_disabled_suppresses_output\n   210→    description: SetColorEnabled(false) causes FileTypeColor and Reset to return empty strings.\n   211→    inputs:\n   212→      function: \"format.FileTypeColor(os.ModeDir) and format.Reset()\"\n   213→      setup: \"format.SetColorEnabled(false)\"\n   214→    expected:\n   215→      file_type_color: '\"\"'\n   216→      reset: '\"\"'\n   217→    traces:\n   218→      - prd003-format R2.6\n   219→\n   220→  - name: reset_color_enabled_restores_auto\n   221→    description: ResetColorEnabled clears the override and reverts to TTY detection.\n   222→    inputs:\n   223→      function: \"format.ResetColorEnabled() then format.ColorEnabled(pipe)\"\n   224→      setup: \"format.SetColorEnabled(true); format.ResetColorEnabled()\"\n   225→    expected:\n   226→      color_enabled_pipe: false\n   227→    traces:\n   228→      - prd003-format R2.7\n   229→\n   230→  - name: color_enabled_pipe\n   231→    description: ColorEnabled returns false for an os.Pipe writer.\n   232→    inputs:\n   233→      function: \"format.ColorEnabled(pipeWriter)\"\n   234→    expected:\n   235→      result: false\n   236→    traces:\n   237→      - prd003-format R2.3\n   238→\n   239→  - name: color_enabled_non_file\n   240→    description: ColorEnabled returns false for a bytes.Buffer (non-*os.File).\n   241→    inputs:\n   242→      function: \"format.ColorEnabled(&bytes.Buffer{})\"\n   243→    expected:\n   244→      result: false\n   245→    traces:\n   246→      - prd003-format R2.3\n   247→\n   248→  - name: human_size_binary\n   249→    description: HumanSize converts bytes using 1024-based units.\n   250→    inputs:\n   251→      function: \"format.HumanSize(1536, HumanSizeOpts{Binary: true})\"\n   252→    expected:\n   253→      result: '\"1.5K\"'\n   254→    traces:\n   255→      - prd003-format R3.1\n   256→      - prd003-format R3.2\n   257→      - prd003-format R3.3\n   258→\n   259→  - name: human_size_si\n   260→    description: HumanSize converts bytes using 1000-based SI units.\n   261→    inputs:\n   262→      function: \"format.HumanSize(1000000, HumanSizeOpts{Binary: false})\"\n   263→    expected:\n   264→      result: '\"1.0MB\"'\n   265→    traces:\n   266→      - prd003-format R3.1\n   267→      - prd003-format R3.2\n   268→\n   269→  - name: human_size_zero\n   270→    description: HumanSize returns \"0\" for zero bytes.\n   271→    inputs:\n   272→      function: \"format.HumanSize(0, HumanSizeOpts{Binary: true})\"\n   273→    expected:\n   274→      result: '\"0\"'\n   275→    traces:\n   276→      - prd003-format R3.4\n   277→\n   278→  # --- uc003-sys: pkg/sys syscall abstractions ---\n   279→\n   280→  - name: terminal_width_pipe\n   281→    description: TerminalWidth returns an error when stdout is a pipe.\n   282→    inputs:\n   283→      function: \"sys.TerminalWidth() with stdout redirected to a pipe\"\n   284→    expected:\n   285→      error: non-nil\n   286→    traces:\n   287→      - prd002-sys R1.1\n   288→\n   289→  - name: is_terminal_pipe_fd\n   290→    description: IsTerminal returns false for a pipe file descriptor.\n   291→    inputs:\n   292→      function: \"sys.IsTerminal(pipeFd)\"\n   293→    expected:\n   294→      result: false\n   295→    traces:\n   296→      - prd002-sys R1.3\n   297→\n   298→  - name: stat_known_file\n   299→    description: Stat returns correct extended metadata for a known file.\n   300→    inputs:\n   301→      function: \"sys.Stat(testFile)\"\n   302→    expected:\n   303→      postcondition: Nlink, Uid, Gid, Dev, Ino, Blocks match stat(1) output\n   304→    traces:\n   305→      - prd002-sys R2.1\n   306→      - prd002-sys R2.2\n   307→\n   308→  - name: lstat_symlink\n   309→    description: Lstat on a symlink returns symlink type bits, not target's mode.\n   310→    inputs:\n   311→      function: \"sys.Lstat(symlinkPath)\"\n   312→    expected:\n   313→      postcondition: \"Mode & os.ModeSymlink != 0\"\n   314→    traces:\n   315→      - prd002-sys R2.1\n   316→\n   317→  - name: stat_modtime_populated\n   318→    description: Stat returns a non-zero ModTime from the platform-correct field.\n   319→    inputs:\n   320→      function: \"sys.Stat(testFile)\"\n   321→    expected:\n   322→      postcondition: ModTime is non-zero and close to os.Stat(testFile).ModTime()\n   323→    traces:\n   324→      - prd002-sys R2.3\n   325→\n   326→  - name: stat_hard_link_count\n   327→    description: Stat returns correct Nlink for a file with multiple hard links.\n   328→    inputs:\n   329→      setup: \"create testFile, create hard link to testFile\"\n   330→      function: \"sys.Stat(testFile)\"\n   331→    expected:\n   332→      postcondition: Nlink == 2\n   333→    traces:\n   334→      - prd002-sys R2.2\n   335→\n   336→  - name: sigpipe_handler_exit_zero\n   337→    description: InstallSIGPIPEHandler causes exit 0 on broken pipe.\n   338→    inputs:\n   339→      function: \"sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() then write to closed pipe\"\n   340→    expected:\n   341→      exit_code: 0\n   342→    traces:\n   343→      - prd002-sys R1.5\n   344→\n   345→  - name: on_terminal_resize_callback\n   346→    description: OnTerminalResize registers a callback invoked on SIGWINCH.\n   347→    inputs:\n   348→      function: \"sys.OnTerminalResize(callback) then send SIGWINCH\"\n   349→    expected:\n   350→      postcondition: callback was called with a positive width\n   351→    traces:\n   352→      - prd002-sys R3.1\n   353→      - prd002-sys R3.2\n   354→\n   355→  - name: cross_platform_compile\n   356→    description: All pkg/sys code compiles on darwin/arm64 and linux/amd64.\n   357→    inputs:\n   358→      command: \"GOOS=darwin GOARCH=arm64 go build ./pkg/sys/ && GOOS=linux GOARCH=amd64 go build ./pkg/sys/\"\n   359→    expected:\n   360→      exit_code: 0\n   361→    traces:\n   362→      - prd002-sys R1.2\n   363→\n   364→  # --- uc004-testutils: pkg/testutils differential testing harness ---\n   365→\n   366→  - name: difftest_zero_value\n   367→    description: DiffTest zero value is a valid test case that runs without error.\n   368→    inputs:\n   369→      function: \"testutils.RunDiffTests(t, echoBin, echoBin, []DiffTest{{Name: \\\"zero\\\"}})\"\n   370→    expected:\n   371→      postcondition: test passes with no output\n   372→    traces:\n   373→      - prd001-testutils R1.1\n   374→\n   375→  - name: rundifftests_matching_output\n   376→    description: RunDiffTests passes silently when both binaries produce identical output.\n   377→    inputs:\n   378→      function: \"testutils.RunDiffTests(t, goBin, refBin, tests)\"\n   379→      setup: \"both mock binaries print \\\"hello\\\\n\\\" to stdout and exit 0\"\n   380→    expected:\n   381→      postcondition: test passes with no output to stdout or stderr\n   382→    traces:\n   383→      - prd001-testutils R2.1\n   384→      - prd001-testutils R2.4\n   385→      - prd001-testutils R3.2\n   386→\n   387→  - name: rundifftests_divergent_stdout\n   388→    description: RunDiffTests reports failure when stdout differs between binaries.\n   389→    inputs:\n   390→      function: \"testutils.RunDiffTests(t, goBin, refBin, tests)\"\n   391→      setup: \"Go binary prints \\\"hello\\\\n\\\", reference prints \\\"world\\\\n\\\"\"\n   392→    expected:\n   393→      postcondition: \"test fails with message containing args, stdin, both stdouts, both stderrs, and both exit codes\"\n   394→    traces:\n   395→      - prd001-testutils R3.2\n   396→      - prd001-testutils R3.5\n   397→\n   398→  - name: rundifftests_divergent_exit_code\n   399→    description: RunDiffTests reports failure when exit codes differ.\n   400→    inputs:\n   401→      function: \"testutils.RunDiffTests(t, goBin, refBin, tests)\"\n   402→      setup: \"Go binary exits 0, reference exits 1\"\n   403→    expected:\n   404→      postcondition: test fails with message showing both exit codes\n   405→    traces:\n   406→      - prd001-testutils R3.4\n   407→      - prd001-testutils R3.5\n   408→\n   409→  - name: timestamp_normalizer\n   410→    description: TimestampNormalizer strips strftime timestamps so time-dependent outputs match.\n   411→    inputs:\n   412→      function: \"testutils.RunDiffTests with Normalize: []NormalizeFunc{TimestampNormalizer}\"\n   413→      setup: \"binaries produce outputs differing only in timestamp field\"\n   414→    expected:\n   415→      postcondition: test passes after normalization strips timestamps\n   416→    traces:\n   417→      - prd001-testutils R4.1\n   418→      - prd001-testutils R4.2\n   419→\n   420→  - name: compose_normalizers\n   421→    description: ComposeNormalizers chains multiple functions applied in order.\n   422→    inputs:\n   423→      function: \"testutils.ComposeNormalizers(fn1, fn2)\"\n   424→    expected:\n   425→      postcondition: \"composed function applies fn1 then fn2; output matches fn2(fn1(input))\"\n   426→    traces:\n   427→      - prd001-testutils R4.3\n   428→      - prd001-testutils R4.4\n   429→\n   430→  - name: expected_files_match\n   431→    description: ExpectedFiles comparison passes when Go binary writes correct file content.\n   432→    inputs:\n   433→      function: \"testutils.RunDiffTests with ExpectedFiles\"\n   434→      setup: \"mock binary writes expected content to output file\"\n   435→    expected:\n   436→      postcondition: test passes with no output\n   437→    traces:\n   438→      - prd001-testutils R5.1\n   439→      - prd001-testutils R5.2\n   440→\n   441→  - name: expected_files_diverge\n   442→    description: ExpectedFiles comparison fails when Go binary writes wrong file content.\n   443→    inputs:\n   444→      function: \"testutils.RunDiffTests with ExpectedFiles\"\n   445→      setup: \"mock binary writes different content than expected\"\n   446→    expected:\n   447→      postcondition: test fails with message showing file path, expected content, actual content\n   448→    traces:\n   449→      - prd001-testutils R5.1\n   450→      - prd001-testutils R5.2\n   451→\n   452→  - name: timeout_exceeded\n   453→    description: RunDiffTests fails with timeout message when binary exceeds timeout.\n   454→    inputs:\n   455→      function: \"testutils.RunDiffTests with short timeout\"\n   456→      setup: \"mock binary sleeps beyond timeout\"\n   457→    expected:\n   458→      postcondition: test fails with clear timeout message, not a hang\n   459→    traces:\n   460→      - prd001-testutils R2.3\n   461→\n   462→  - name: env_default_lc_all\n   463→    description: RunDiffTests sets LC_ALL=C by default when DiffTest.Env is nil.\n   464→    inputs:\n   465→      function: \"testutils.RunDiffTests with Env: nil\"\n   466→      setup: \"mock binary prints LC_ALL environment variable\"\n   467→    expected:\n   468→      postcondition: \"binary receives LC_ALL=C\"\n   469→    traces:\n   470→      - prd001-testutils R2.6\n   471→\n   472→  - name: env_override_lc_all\n   473→    description: DiffTest.Env overrides the LC_ALL=C default.\n   474→    inputs:\n   475→      function: 'testutils.RunDiffTests with Env: []string{\"LC_ALL=en_US.UTF-8\"}'\n   476→      setup: \"mock binary prints LC_ALL environment variable\"\n   477→    expected:\n   478→      postcondition: \"binary receives LC_ALL=en_US.UTF-8\"\n   479→    traces:\n   480→      - prd001-testutils R1.3\n   481→      - prd001-testutils R2.6\n   482→\n   483→  - name: workdir_default_tempdir\n   484→    description: WorkDir defaults to t.TempDir() when empty.\n   485→    inputs:\n   486→      function: \"testutils.RunDiffTests with WorkDir: \\\"\\\"\"\n   487→      setup: \"mock binary prints working directory\"\n   488→    expected:\n   489→      postcondition: both binaries run in a temporary directory\n   490→    traces:\n   491→      - prd001-testutils R2.5\n   492→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4cd21a5e-992a-4826-9906-d82e92835cb4","uuid":"599c1a9a-e074-45d9-8fe4-b78e3cef5a9b","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/232/docs/specs/test-suites/test-rel00.0.yaml","content":"id: test-rel00.0\ntitle: \"Test Suite: rel00.0 — build lifecycle, cmd/version, pkg/format, pkg/sys, and pkg/testutils\"\nrelease: rel00.0\n\ntraces:\n  - rel00.0-uc001-magefiles\n  - prd011-magefiles R1\n  - prd011-magefiles R2\n  - prd011-magefiles R3\n  - prd011-magefiles R4\n  - rel00.0-uc002-format\n  - prd003-format R1\n  - prd003-format R2\n  - prd003-format R3\n  - rel00.0-uc003-sys\n  - prd002-sys R1\n  - prd002-sys R2\n  - prd002-sys R3\n  - rel00.0-uc004-testutils\n  - prd001-testutils R1\n  - prd001-testutils R2\n  - prd001-testutils R3\n  - prd001-testutils R4\n  - prd001-testutils R5\n\npreconditions:\n  - A git repository with at least one tag matching v[0-9]*.\n  - golangci-lint is installed and on PATH.\n  - mage is installed and on PATH.\n  - configuration.yaml exists at the project root with project.binary_dir set to bin.\n\ntest_cases:\n  # --- uc001-magefiles: build lifecycle and version ---\n\n  - name: build_produces_version_binary\n    description: mage build compiles cmd/version to bin/version.\n    inputs:\n      command: \"mage build\"\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n      postcondition: bin/version exists and is executable\n    traces:\n      - prd011-magefiles R2.1\n      - prd011-magefiles R2.4\n\n  - name: version_prints_tag\n    description: bin/version prints the latest version tag and exits 0.\n    inputs:\n      command: \"bin/version\"\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n      stdout_pattern: \"v[0-9]+\\\\.[0-9]+\\\\.[0-9]+\\n\"\n      stderr: \"\"\n    traces:\n      - prd011-magefiles R1.1\n      - prd011-magefiles R1.2\n      - prd011-magefiles R1.3\n\n  - name: version_flag_identical\n    description: bin/version --version produces the same output as bin/version.\n    inputs:\n      command: \"bin/version --version\"\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n      stdout: same as bin/version with no arguments\n      stderr: \"\"\n    traces:\n      - prd011-magefiles R1.4\n\n  - name: version_v_flag_identical\n    description: bin/version -v produces the same output as bin/version.\n    inputs:\n      command: \"bin/version -v\"\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n      stdout: same as bin/version with no arguments\n      stderr: \"\"\n    traces:\n      - prd011-magefiles R1.4\n\n  - name: version_unknown_flag\n    description: bin/version --bogus prints usage to stderr and exits 2.\n    inputs:\n      command: \"bin/version --bogus\"\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 2\n      stdout: \"\"\n      stderr_contains: \"usage\"\n    traces:\n      - prd011-magefiles R1.4\n\n  - name: version_dev_build\n    description: A build without ldflags prints \"dev\".\n    inputs:\n      command: \"go build -o /tmp/version-dev ./cmd/version && /tmp/version-dev\"\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n      stdout: \"dev\\n\"\n      stderr: \"\"\n    traces:\n      - prd011-magefiles R1.2\n\n  - name: lint_passes\n    description: mage lint runs golangci-lint with no warnings.\n    inputs:\n      command: \"mage lint\"\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n    traces:\n      - prd011-magefiles R3.1\n      - prd011-magefiles R3.2\n\n  - name: clean_removes_bin\n    description: mage clean removes the bin/ directory.\n    inputs:\n      command: \"mage build && mage clean\"\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n      postcondition: bin/ does not exist\n    traces:\n      - prd011-magefiles R4.1\n\n  - name: clean_idempotent\n    description: mage clean succeeds when bin/ does not exist.\n    inputs:\n      command: \"mage clean && mage clean\"\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n    traces:\n      - prd011-magefiles R4.1\n\n  # --- uc002-format: pkg/format output formatting ---\n\n  - name: columns_20_entries_80_width\n    description: Columns distributes 20 filenames into a grid fitting 80 columns.\n    inputs:\n      function: \"format.Columns(entries, 80)\"\n      entries: 20 filenames of varying lengths\n    expected:\n      postcondition: each row fits within 80 characters; column count is maximized\n    traces:\n      - prd003-format R1.1\n      - prd003-format R1.4\n\n  - name: columns_empty_input\n    description: Columns returns nil for empty input.\n    inputs:\n      function: \"format.Columns(nil, 80)\"\n    expected:\n      result: nil\n    traces:\n      - prd003-format R1.1\n\n  - name: pad_right\n    description: PadRight pads a string with trailing spaces to the specified width.\n    inputs:\n      function: 'format.PadRight(\"abc\", 10)'\n    expected:\n      result: '\"abc       \"  (abc + 7 spaces)'\n    traces:\n      - prd003-format R1.2\n\n  - name: pad_left\n    description: PadLeft pads a string with leading spaces to the specified width.\n    inputs:\n      function: 'format.PadLeft(\"abc\", 10)'\n    expected:\n      result: '\"       abc\"  (7 spaces + abc)'\n    traces:\n      - prd003-format R1.2\n\n  - name: display_width_multibyte\n    description: Column width uses rune count, not byte count, for multi-byte strings.\n    inputs:\n      function: 'format.PadRight(\"café\", 10)'\n    expected:\n      result: '\"café      \"  (café is 4 runes, padded to 10)'\n    traces:\n      - prd003-format R1.3\n\n  - name: file_type_color_all_types\n    description: FileTypeColor returns correct ANSI codes for all eight file types.\n    inputs:\n      function: \"format.FileTypeColor(mode) for each file type\"\n      setup: \"format.SetColorEnabled(true)\"\n    expected:\n      directory: '\"\\033[34m\"'\n      symlink: '\"\\033[36m\"'\n      executable: '\"\\033[32m\"'\n      block_device: '\"\\033[33;1m\"'\n      char_device: '\"\\033[33;1m\"'\n      socket: '\"\\033[35m\"'\n      pipe: '\"\\033[33m\"'\n      regular: '\"\"'\n    traces:\n      - prd003-format R2.1\n      - prd003-format R2.4\n\n  - name: reset_color_enabled\n    description: Reset returns ANSI reset sequence when color is enabled.\n    inputs:\n      function: \"format.Reset()\"\n      setup: \"format.SetColorEnabled(true)\"\n    expected:\n      result: '\"\\033[0m\"'\n    traces:\n      - prd003-format R2.2\n\n  - name: color_disabled_suppresses_output\n    description: SetColorEnabled(false) causes FileTypeColor and Reset to return empty strings.\n    inputs:\n      function: \"format.FileTypeColor(os.ModeDir) and format.Reset()\"\n      setup: \"format.SetColorEnabled(false)\"\n    expected:\n      file_type_color: '\"\"'\n      reset: '\"\"'\n    traces:\n      - prd003-format R2.6\n\n  - name: reset_color_enabled_restores_auto\n    description: ResetColorEnabled clears the override and reverts to TTY detection.\n    inputs:\n      function: \"format.ResetColorEnabled() then format.ColorEnabled(pipe)\"\n      setup: \"format.SetColorEnabled(true); format.ResetColorEnabled()\"\n    expected:\n      color_enabled_pipe: false\n    traces:\n      - prd003-format R2.7\n\n  - name: color_enabled_pipe\n    description: ColorEnabled returns false for an os.Pipe writer.\n    inputs:\n      function: \"format.ColorEnabled(pipeWriter)\"\n    expected:\n      result: false\n    traces:\n      - prd003-format R2.3\n\n  - name: color_enabled_non_file\n    description: ColorEnabled returns false for a bytes.Buffer (non-*os.File).\n    inputs:\n      function: \"format.ColorEnabled(&bytes.Buffer{})\"\n    expected:\n      result: false\n    traces:\n      - prd003-format R2.3\n\n  - name: human_size_binary\n    description: HumanSize converts bytes using 1024-based units.\n    inputs:\n      function: \"format.HumanSize(1536, HumanSizeOpts{Binary: true})\"\n    expected:\n      result: '\"1.5K\"'\n    traces:\n      - prd003-format R3.1\n      - prd003-format R3.2\n      - prd003-format R3.3\n\n  - name: human_size_si\n    description: HumanSize converts bytes using 1000-based SI units.\n    inputs:\n      function: \"format.HumanSize(1000000, HumanSizeOpts{Binary: false})\"\n    expected:\n      result: '\"1.0MB\"'\n    traces:\n      - prd003-format R3.1\n      - prd003-format R3.2\n\n  - name: human_size_zero\n    description: HumanSize returns \"0\" for zero bytes.\n    inputs:\n      function: \"format.HumanSize(0, HumanSizeOpts{Binary: true})\"\n    expected:\n      result: '\"0\"'\n    traces:\n      - prd003-format R3.4\n\n  # --- uc003-sys: pkg/sys syscall abstractions ---\n\n  - name: terminal_width_pipe\n    description: TerminalWidth returns an error when stdout is a pipe.\n    inputs:\n      function: \"sys.TerminalWidth() with stdout redirected to a pipe\"\n    expected:\n      error: non-nil\n    traces:\n      - prd002-sys R1.1\n\n  - name: is_terminal_pipe_fd\n    description: IsTerminal returns false for a pipe file descriptor.\n    inputs:\n      function: \"sys.IsTerminal(pipeFd)\"\n    expected:\n      result: false\n    traces:\n      - prd002-sys R1.3\n\n  - name: stat_known_file\n    description: Stat returns correct extended metadata for a known file.\n    inputs:\n      function: \"sys.Stat(testFile)\"\n    expected:\n      postcondition: Nlink, Uid, Gid, Dev, Ino, Blocks match stat(1) output\n    traces:\n      - prd002-sys R2.1\n      - prd002-sys R2.2\n\n  - name: lstat_symlink\n    description: Lstat on a symlink returns symlink type bits, not target's mode.\n    inputs:\n      function: \"sys.Lstat(symlinkPath)\"\n    expected:\n      postcondition: \"Mode & os.ModeSymlink != 0\"\n    traces:\n      - prd002-sys R2.1\n\n  - name: stat_modtime_populated\n    description: Stat returns a non-zero ModTime from the platform-correct field.\n    inputs:\n      function: \"sys.Stat(testFile)\"\n    expected:\n      postcondition: ModTime is non-zero and close to os.Stat(testFile).ModTime()\n    traces:\n      - prd002-sys R2.3\n\n  - name: stat_hard_link_count\n    description: Stat returns correct Nlink for a file with multiple hard links.\n    inputs:\n      setup: \"create testFile, create hard link to testFile\"\n      function: \"sys.Stat(testFile)\"\n    expected:\n      postcondition: Nlink == 2\n    traces:\n      - prd002-sys R2.2\n\n  - name: sigpipe_handler_exit_zero\n    description: InstallSIGPIPEHandler causes exit 0 on broken pipe.\n    inputs:\n      function: \"sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() then write to closed pipe\"\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n    traces:\n      - prd002-sys R1.5\n\n  - name: on_terminal_resize_callback\n    description: OnTerminalResize registers a callback invoked on SIGWINCH.\n    inputs:\n      function: \"sys.OnTerminalResize(callback) then send SIGWINCH\"\n    expected:\n      postcondition: callback was called with a positive width\n    traces:\n      - prd002-sys R3.1\n      - prd002-sys R3.2\n\n  - name: cross_platform_compile\n    description: All pkg/sys code compiles on darwin/arm64 and linux/amd64.\n    inputs:\n      command: \"GOOS=darwin GOARCH=arm64 go build ./pkg/sys/ && GOOS=linux GOARCH=amd64 go build ./pkg/sys/\"\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n    traces:\n      - prd002-sys R1.2\n\n  # --- uc004-testutils: pkg/testutils differential testing harness ---\n\n  - name: difftest_zero_value\n    description: DiffTest zero value is a valid test case that runs without error.\n    inputs:\n      function: \"testutils.RunDiffTests(t, echoBin, echoBin, []DiffTest{{Name: \\\"zero\\\"}})\"\n    expected:\n      postcondition: test passes with no output\n    traces:\n      - prd001-testutils R1.1\n\n  - name: rundifftests_matching_output\n    description: RunDiffTests passes silently when both binaries produce identical output.\n    inputs:\n      function: \"testutils.RunDiffTests(t, goBin, refBin, tests)\"\n      setup: \"both mock binaries print \\\"hello\\\\n\\\" to stdout and exit 0\"\n    expected:\n      postcondition: test passes with no output to stdout or stderr\n    traces:\n      - prd001-testutils R2.1\n      - prd001-testutils R2.4\n      - prd001-testutils R3.2\n\n  - name: rundifftests_divergent_stdout\n    description: RunDiffTests reports failure when stdout differs between binaries.\n    inputs:\n      function: \"testutils.RunDiffTests(t, goBin, refBin, tests)\"\n      setup: \"Go binary prints \\\"hello\\\\n\\\", reference prints \\\"world\\\\n\\\"\"\n    expected:\n      postcondition: \"test fails with message containing args, stdin, both stdouts, both stderrs, and both exit codes\"\n    traces:\n      - prd001-testutils R3.2\n      - prd001-testutils R3.5\n\n  - name: rundifftests_divergent_exit_code\n    description: RunDiffTests reports failure when exit codes differ.\n    inputs:\n      function: \"testutils.RunDiffTests(t, goBin, refBin, tests)\"\n      setup: \"Go binary exits 0, reference exits 1\"\n    expected:\n      postcondition: test fails with message showing both exit codes\n    traces:\n      - prd001-testutils R3.4\n      - prd001-testutils R3.5\n\n  - name: timestamp_normalizer\n    description: TimestampNormalizer strips strftime timestamps so time-dependent outputs match.\n    inputs:\n      function: \"testutils.RunDiffTests with Normalize: []NormalizeFunc{TimestampNormalizer}\"\n      setup: \"binaries produce outputs differing only in timestamp field\"\n    expected:\n      postcondition: test passes after normalization strips timestamps\n    traces:\n      - prd001-testutils R4.1\n      - prd001-testutils R4.2\n\n  - name: compose_normalizers\n    description: ComposeNormalizers chains multiple functions applied in order.\n    inputs:\n      function: \"testutils.ComposeNormalizers(fn1, fn2)\"\n    expected:\n      postcondition: \"composed function applies fn1 then fn2; output matches fn2(fn1(input))\"\n    traces:\n      - prd001-testutils R4.3\n      - prd001-testutils R4.4\n\n  - name: expected_files_match\n    description: ExpectedFiles comparison passes when Go binary writes correct file content.\n    inputs:\n      function: \"testutils.RunDiffTests with ExpectedFiles\"\n      setup: \"mock binary writes expected content to output file\"\n    expected:\n      postcondition: test passes with no output\n    traces:\n      - prd001-testutils R5.1\n      - prd001-testutils R5.2\n\n  - name: expected_files_diverge\n    description: ExpectedFiles comparison fails when Go binary writes wrong file content.\n    inputs:\n      function: \"testutils.RunDiffTests with ExpectedFiles\"\n      setup: \"mock binary writes different content than expected\"\n    expected:\n      postcondition: test fails with message showing file path, expected content, actual content\n    traces:\n      - prd001-testutils R5.1\n      - prd001-testutils R5.2\n\n  - name: timeout_exceeded\n    description: RunDiffTests fails with timeout message when binary exceeds timeout.\n    inputs:\n      function: \"testutils.RunDiffTests with short timeout\"\n      setup: \"mock binary sleeps beyond timeout\"\n    expected:\n      postcondition: test fails with clear timeout message, not a hang\n    traces:\n      - prd001-testutils R2.3\n\n  - name: env_default_lc_all\n    description: RunDiffTests sets LC_ALL=C by default when DiffTest.Env is nil.\n    inputs:\n      function: \"testutils.RunDiffTests with Env: nil\"\n      setup: \"mock binary prints LC_ALL environment variable\"\n    expected:\n      postcondition: \"binary receives LC_ALL=C\"\n    traces:\n      - prd001-testutils R2.6\n\n  - name: env_override_lc_all\n    description: DiffTest.Env overrides the LC_ALL=C default.\n    inputs:\n      function: 'testutils.RunDiffTests with Env: []string{\"LC_ALL=en_US.UTF-8\"}'\n      setup: \"mock binary prints LC_ALL environment variable\"\n    expected:\n      postcondition: \"binary receives LC_ALL=en_US.UTF-8\"\n    traces:\n      - prd001-testutils R1.3\n      - prd001-testutils R2.6\n\n  - name: workdir_default_tempdir\n    description: WorkDir defaults to t.TempDir() when empty.\n    inputs:\n      function: \"testutils.RunDiffTests with WorkDir: \\\"\\\"\"\n      setup: \"mock binary prints working directory\"\n    expected:\n      postcondition: both binaries run in a temporary directory\n    traces:\n      - prd001-testutils R2.5\n","numLines":492,"startLine":1,"totalLines":492}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01TfSDUyFTyBovTuJc9qNfBf","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a clear picture. Let me note something important: the existing code in `pkg/sys/fileinfo.go` has a `FileMetadata` struct with unexported fields (`modTime`, `blocks`) and accessors `ModTime()` and `Blocks()`. The PRD R2.2 describes a `FileInfo` struct with exported fields, but the actual implementation uses `FileMetadata` with unexported fields. I need to test what actually exists, not what the PRD describes as the future target.\n\nLooking at the existing source code:\n- `FileMetadata` struct with `modTime time.Time` and `blocks int64` (unexported)\n\n\n- Accessor methods for `ModTime()` and `Blocks()`\n- `Stat()` and `Lstat()` functions that return `FileMetadata` pointers\n\nSo my test cases need to cover: verifying that `Stat` correctly retrieves metadata for regular files and follows symlinks, `Lstat` returns the symlink's own metadata without following it, the accessor methods return accurate values matching the filesystem, and both functions properly handle error cases like missing files.\n\nI'm setting up the test file with the right build tags and external package structure, using temporary directories and symlinks to create test fixtures. Now I need to write out the actual test implementations, being careful to use the correct `FileMetadata` type from the existing implementation.\n\nWriting test functions... For Lstat, since symlinks typically allocate minimal blocks and might have different modification times than their targets, I can verify the distinction by creating a file with content, making a symlink to it, and confirming that Stat returns matching ModTime and Blocks while Lstat would show different values for the symlink itself. I'm settling on a cleaner approach: create a file with an mtime set to an hour ago, then create a symlink pointing to it. When I call Stat on the symlink, it should follow the link and return the file's older mtime, but Lstat should return the symlink's own recent creation time. For R5, I need to verify that both Stat and Lstat return errors for non-existent paths and that those errors include the path information.\n\nWriting the test file... Writing test code... Writing test code... Writing test assertions... Writing test assertions... Writing test functions... Writing test functions... Checking off the remaining requirements and acceptance criteria—I've covered the error handling tests and symlink behavior, verified the time and blocks constraints are within acceptable ranges, and confirmed the build tag and package structure are correct. , these tests together demonstrate how Stat follows symlinks while Lstat doesn't. Now I'm ready to write out the test file.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9288,"cache_read_input_tokens":44865,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":9288},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4cd21a5e-992a-4826-9906-d82e92835cb4","uuid":"de761caf-43e7-4ce3-a689-8bf675f449ca"}
